# Line endings.
#
# Every text file in this repository uses LF today: the Python, the Markdown, the
# JavaScript, the CSS, the HTML, the JSON, the PowerShell and this file. The first commit
# therefore records LF, and the line below holds it there.
#
# Without this file a contributor on Windows with the Git default `core.autocrlf=true`
# checks out CRLF and commits CRLF back. Every line of every file they touch then shows as
# a change, and the repair rewrites every file and breaks every blame and every diff.
# This is the only cheap moment to set it: after the first commit, a change here rewrites
# the working tree of everybody.
* text=auto eol=lf

# `ops/register-tasks.ps1` is LF today, and both PowerShell 5.1 and PowerShell 7 run an LF
# script, so the rule above is correct for it. A team that wants the Windows convention
# for PowerShell adds `*.ps1 text eol=crlf` here instead. Decide once.

# Binary. Never convert an end of line in these, and never attempt a text diff.
*.png    binary
*.webp   binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.ico    binary
*.gz     binary
*.zip    binary
*.duckdb binary
*.pyc    binary
